<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Christian August, Prince of Anhalt-Zerbst</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1690">1690</a>–<a href="/years/1747">1747</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Christian August, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, was a German prince and a general in Prussian service who governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>. He became joint ruler of Anhalt-Zerbst with his brother in 1742 and sole prince shortly before his death in 1747. His daughter Sophie, born in Stettin in 1729, married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-iii-of-russia">Peter III of Russia</a> and became Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine the Great</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/christian-august-prince-of-anhalt-zerbst.jpg" alt="Christian August, Prince of Anhalt-Zerbst" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoine Pesne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Christian_August_of_Anhalt-Zerbst,_1725_(Antoine_Pesne).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Christian Augustus was born at Dornburg on 29 November 1690, the third son of John Louis I, Prince of Anhalt-Dornburg, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-ascania">House of Ascania</a>, and Christine Eleonore of Zeutsch. When his father died in 1704 he inherited Anhalt-Dornburg jointly with his brothers. On 8 November 1727, at Vechelde, he married Johanna Elisabeth of Holstein-Gottorp, a sister of the future King Adolf Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. They had five children, of whom the eldest, Sophie Auguste Fredericka, born on 2 May 1729, became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catherine-the-great">Catherine II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; two of the others died in infancy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On 11 February 1709 he joined the Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a> infantry regiment, stationed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a>, in Prussian service. He received the Order De la Générosité, later renamed Pour le Mérite, in 1711, was made lieutenant-colonel on 1 March 1713, and took part in several campaigns of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> in the Spanish Netherlands. In 1714 he became chief of the regiment, and he rose to colonel in 1716 and major-general in 1721.</p><p>He was made a knight of the Order of the Black Eagle in 1725 and became commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> on 22 January 1729. Promoted lieutenant-general in 1732 and general of infantry in 1741, he was named Governor of Stettin on 5 June 1741. On 16 May 1742 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-the-great">Frederick the Great</a> gave him the highest military rank, Generalfeldmarschall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>About six months later his cousin John Augustus, Prince of Anhalt-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, died without children, and Christian Augustus and his only surviving brother, John Louis II, inherited the principality as co-rulers. Christian Augustus stayed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szczecin">Stettin</a> while his brother governed. When John Louis II died, unmarried and childless, Christian Augustus left Stettin for Zerbst, but he ruled alone for only four months before his own death there on 16 March 1747.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Christian Augustus, Prince of Anhalt-Zerbst” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its most famous member was Sophie Auguste Friederike, daughter of Prince <b>Christian August</b>, who married the future Peter III and became Empress Catherine II of Russia.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-zerbst">Principality of Anhalt-Zerbst</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Dornburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zerbst">Zerbst</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.04,11.88&amp;year=1727" aria-label="Dornburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4818 366 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Christian August, Prince of Anhalt-Zerbst"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 636h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 636h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.04,11.88&amp;year=1727">What was here in 1727? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
